  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are on a one-dimensional number line, initially at x=1x = 1x=1. You want to reach x=N+1x = N + 1x=N+1.\n\nYou can do the following 222 actions when you are at x(x≤N)x (x \\le N)x(x≤N):\n\n\nPay a cost of AxA_xAx​ and move to x+1x + 1x+1.\nPay a cost of BxB_xBx​ and move to a random position among the positions x=1,2,…,N+1x = 1, 2, \\ldots, N + 1x=1,2,…,N+1. All of the N+1N + 1N+1 possibilities are equiprobable.\nFind the minimum expected cost to reach N+1N + 1N+1 if we make decisions optimally.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains NNN.\nThe second line contains NNN integers - A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.\nThe third line contains NNN integers - B1,B2,…,BNB_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_NB1​,B2​,…,BN​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the minimum expected cost to reach N+1N + 1N+1.\n\n\nYour answer will be marked correct if either the absolute or the relative error does not exceed 10−610^{-6}10−6.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\le T \\le 10^41≤T≤104",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\le N \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Ai,Bi≤1061 \\le A_i, B_i \\le 10^61≤Ai​,Bi​≤106",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "5\n1\n5\n2\n1\n2\n5\n2\n10 2\n1 10\n3\n1 2 3\n3 2 1\n6\n67 15 44 35 28 11\n22 12 23 12 5 30",
        "output": "4.0\n2.0\n2.5\n6.0\n61.666667",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1 : The optimal strategy is to always use the randomize operation when we are at x=1x = 1x=1. Then, the probability that we reach x=2x = 2x=2 in exactly iii turns can be shown to be 12i\\dfrac{1}{2^i}2i1​ and the associated cost is 2⋅i2 \\cdot i2⋅i.\nSumming up the infinite series, ∑i=1inf⁡2⋅i⋅12i\\sum_{i = 1}^{\\inf} 2 \\cdot i \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2^i}∑i=1inf​2⋅i⋅2i1​, we get 444.\nTest Case 2 : The optimal strategy is just to use the normal operation of going to x+1x + 1x+1 when we are at x=1x = 1x=1. The guaranteed cost of this operation is 222."
      }
